---
import X from '../../data/day2-embedding.json';
const P = X.panel;
const F = P.fc;
const pct = (v: number) => Math.round(100 * v);
const f = (v: number, d = 0) => v.toLocaleString('en-US', { maximumFractionDigits: d, minimumFractionDigits: d });
const mi = (m: string) => F.mean[F.models.indexOf(m)];
const never = P.m_series.never / P.n;
const neverNoise = P.m_noise.never / P.n_noise;
const tau1 = P.tau_hist['1'] / P.n;
const tauLe4 = ['1', '2', '3', '4'].reduce((z, k) => z + (P.tau_hist[k] || 0), 0) / P.n;
const pooled = P.pooled?.best;
const SH = P.shuffle;
const AN = P.analogs;
---
<section class="d2 part">
  <div class="plate">
    <h3>Why can we forecast these series at all?</h3>
    <p>A forecast is a bet that the future will resemble what followed similar situations in the past. Delay embeddings make
      that idea visible. Takens (1981) proved that, for a smooth deterministic system on a compact manifold of dimension <i>d</i>,
      2<i>d</i>+1 delayed copies of a single generic observation, (x(t), x(t−τ), …, x(t−2dτ)), form an <i>embedding</i>: a one-to-one,
      smooth picture of the hidden state (Packard et al. 1980 had shown the reconstruction in practice). In that picture each week
      is a point, the "state" of the series, and if nearby states have similar futures, the series is predictable. This page
      looks at the workshop data that way: {f(P.n)} weekly series, 93 training weeks each. Retail sales are not a clean deterministic
      system, so we use the pictures to look for structure, not to prove the theorem applies.</p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>{SH ? pct(SH.share_gt) : '–'} %</b><span>of series predict their own next week better than with the values shuffled (chance: 50 %); {SH ? pct(SH.share_gt_q90) : '–'} % beat 9 in 10 shuffles (chance: 10 %)</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{SH ? f(SH.med_real, 2) : '–'}</b><span>median skill ρ from a series' own past states (shuffled: {SH ? f(SH.med_shuf, 2) : '–'})</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{pct(P.smap_sig_all)} %</b><span>show state-dependent (nonlinear) structure beyond linear surrogates (5 % by chance)</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{AN ? f(AN.n_windows) : '–'}</b><span>26-week windows from all items: a shared library of past situations to look up</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="take">Short answer: yes, there are patterns. Most are weak inside a single item and become usable when you pool across items. In most series,
      states that look alike are followed by weeks that look alike more often than chance (section 2), and when you search
      <i>all</i> items for windows that look like this item's recent weeks, their continuations give a sensible range for its next weeks (section 3).
      That is why a global model trained on every series can forecast an item whose own 93 weeks look like noise. The recurrence
      plot (section 4) shows the repeating stretches directly. The classic recipe for a single series, choosing τ and m and
      forecasting from that series' own past, is weak on this data (sections 5 to 7).</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>1 · Build an embedding</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="embSeries"></div></div>
    <div class="ctrl">
      <label>delay τ <input type="range" id="embTau" min="1" max="26" step="1" value="3" /> <b id="embTauV"></b></label>
      <div class="seg" id="embDim"><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="2">2D</button><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="3">3D</button></div>
      <div class="seg" id="embCol"><button aria-pressed="true" data-v="time">colour by time</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="split">train / test</button><button aria-pressed="false" data-v="promo">promotion weeks</button></div>
      <label><input type="checkbox" id="embLog" /> log(1+y)</label>
    </div>
    <div class="fig" id="embTs"></div>
    <div class="ctrl"><label>time t <input type="range" id="embT" min="0" max="106" step="1" /></label><button class="btn" id="embPlay">Play</button></div>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="fig" id="embPortrait" style="touch-action:none;cursor:grab"></div>
      <div>
        <p class="readout" id="embRead"></p>
        <p class="cap">Each week t becomes one point (x(t), x(t−τ), x(t−2τ)): the three dots on the series above. In 3D, drag to rotate.
          Grey lines join consecutive weeks. For the <b>Lorenz, 1,000 pts</b> reference you see the familiar butterfly; a deterministic
          system draws a thin, folded surface. A yearly cycle draws a loop, but with 107 weeks it goes round only twice.</p>
        <p class="take">Retail items make a cloud, not a curve. Promotion weeks (switch the colour) sit far out along each axis in turn:
          a spike travels through x(t), then x(t−τ), then x(t−2τ), which draws a "star" with arms along the axes. That is the price
          calendar, an outside driver, not hidden dynamics.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>2 · Similar states, similar futures</h3>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div>
        <div class="fig" id="embNb"></div>
        <p class="readout" id="embNbRead"></p>
        <p class="cap">Black: the series around "now" (the time slider in section 1; by default the last training week), with the delay
          coordinates as dots. Orange: what followed the m + 1 past states closest to it (orange rings in the portrait), with their own
          run-up in grey. Blue: their weighted average, the simplex forecast (Sugihara and May 1990). Dashed black: what actually happened.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="fig" id="embShuf"></div>
        <p class="readout" id="embShufRead"></p>
        <p class="cap">Is that better than luck? Skill ρ is the correlation between the neighbours' next week and the real next week,
          for training weeks 61 to 93 with weeks 1 to 60 as the library, at the best E. Grey: the same calculation on 19 random
          shufflings of the same values, which keep the values and destroy the order. Picking the best E is optimistic for both, so the
          shuffles are not at zero.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="fig" id="embPShuf"></div>
    <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--a1)"></i>{SH ? f(SH.n) : ''} series, real order</span><span><i style="background:#94a3b8"></i>same series, values shuffled once</span></div>
    <p class="take">{SH ? pct(SH.share_gt) : ''} % of series predict their next week better in real order than shuffled, and the whole
      distribution moves right (median ρ {SH ? f(SH.med_real, 2) : ''} against {SH ? f(SH.med_shuf, 2) : ''}). By type: smooth
      {SH ? pct(SH.by_type.smooth[0]) : ''} %, erratic {SH ? pct(SH.by_type.erratic[0]) : ''} %, intermittent
      {SH ? pct(SH.by_type.intermittent[0]) : ''} %, lumpy {SH ? pct(SH.by_type.lumpy[0]) : ''} %. So the order of the weeks carries
      information: that is what makes forecasting possible. A stricter bar gives the same picture: {SH ? pct(SH.share_gt_q90) : ''} % of series beat
      the 90th percentile of shuffled skill, where chance would give 10 %. It is a modest signal per item, far from the Lorenz and yearly-cycle
      references. The whole-store total fails this test: its level in weeks 61 to 93 is not in the library of weeks 1 to 60, so a trend or level shift
      leaves nearest neighbours with nothing close to look up.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>3 · Similar states in other items</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><div class="seg" id="embAnSel"></div></div>
    <div class="fig" id="embAn"></div>
    <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--ink)"></i>this item (dashed: the 14 test weeks)</span><span><i style="background:#94a3b8"></i>12 closest windows from other items</span><span><i style="background:var(--a2)"></i>what followed them</span><span><i style="background:var(--a1)"></i>weighted average of the {AN ? AN.k : ''} closest (band: 10th to 90th percentile)</span></div>
    <p class="readout" id="embAnRead"></p>
    <div class="tw" id="embAnList"></div>
    <p class="cap">Every item is divided by its own training mean, so a window is a shape, not a level. The library holds every {AN ? AN.L : ''}-week
      training window of every item that still has 14 known weeks after it; the item itself is left out. Nearest neighbours by Euclidean
      distance (scikit-learn), weights exp(−d/d<sub>min</sub>) as in simplex projection. Window length and k were chosen on the validation weeks.
      Orange ticks at the bottom: promotion weeks of this item.</p>
    <p class="take">The neighbours are unrelated products (the list shows the closest few), yet their continuations often bracket what this
      item did next. Scored on all series, this lookup beats both the item's own embedding and "repeat last week" (mean RMSSE
      {f(mi('PooledAnalog'), 3)} against {f(mi('Simplex'), 3)} and {f(mi('Naive'), 3)}; table in section 7). A single item has about 50 past states to compare with; the panel has hundreds of thousands. Delay vectors from many
      series are what a global model sees when it is trained on windows of every item, which is one way to see why the Day 2 global models
      work. Like simplex, the analogs only know sales: promotions and prices, which the trained models also use, are not in the windows.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>4 · Recurrence plot: repeating stretches</h3>
    <div class="ctrl"><label>recurrence rate <input type="range" id="embRR" min="2" max="30" step="1" value="10" /></label></div>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div class="fig"><canvas id="embRP" style="width:100%;max-width:360px;aspect-ratio:1;border:1px solid var(--line)"></canvas></div>
      <div>
        <p class="readout" id="embRpRead"></p>
        <p class="cap">A dot at (i, j) when the embedded states at weeks i and j are within ε of each other, with ε set so that the chosen
          share of pairs counts as close. Eckmann, Kamphorst and Ruelle (1987) introduced the plot (with a radius per point that holds at
          least 10 neighbours; a single ε is the common modern choice). Orange lines: start of the test window. Uses the τ and 2D/3D
          setting from section 1.</p>
        <p class="take">This is a picture of "has the series been here before?" that a line chart cannot give. Blocks are periods spent in one
          state, gaps are weeks unlike anything else, and diagonal lines are stretches of the past that repeat; DET is the share of dots on such lines. Compare it with the
          shuffled series (one random shuffle, same recurrence rate): the references reach 76 to 93 % against about 20 %. The items and
          aggregates are only modestly above their shuffle, and the smooth and intermittent examples not at all. Promotion weeks show as
          isolated rows and columns: they resemble nothing else.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>5 · Choosing τ and m</h3>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div>
        <div class="fig" id="embMI"></div>
        <p class="cap">Blue: average mutual information between x(t) and x(t+τ) (teaspoon, Sturges bins on ranks). Grey dashed: autocorrelation
          (right axis). Fraser and Swinney (1986) proposed taking τ at a minimum of the mutual information, usually the first one.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="fig" id="embFNN"></div>
        <p class="readout" id="embFnnRead"></p>
        <p class="cap">False nearest neighbours (Kennel, Brown and Abarbanel 1992): the share of nearest neighbours in m dimensions that fly apart
          when one more delay is added. Blue: this series. Purple: Lorenz (1,000 points), which drops to zero at m = 3. Grey band:
          white noise of the same length (middle half of {f(P.n_noise)} runs).</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="take">{pct(tauLe4)} % of series get τ ≤ 4 weeks ({pct(tau1)} % get τ = 1): with 93 points the mutual-information curve is noisy, and
      its first dip is usually a wiggle, not the quarter period of a cycle (13 weeks for a yearly one; even the synthetic yearly cycle gets a
      small τ). The typical false-neighbour curve of a retail series sits inside the white-noise band (the median is a few points lower), while
      the Lorenz and yearly-cycle references drop to zero. {pct(1 - never)} % of series do reach 5 % or less by m = 7, against {pct(1 - neverNoise)} %
      of noise runs, so a minority has some structure. For most, in Kennel's terms, no small dimension "unfolds" them.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>6 · Forecasting from a series' own embedding</h3>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div><div class="fig" id="embRhoE"></div><p class="cap">Simplex projection (Sugihara and May 1990, pyEDM): predict next week from the E+1 nearest past states, weighted by exp(−d/d<sub>min</sub>). Skill ρ = correlation of prediction and outcome on training weeks 61–93, library weeks 1–60.</p></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embRhoTp"></div><p class="cap">Skill against steps ahead at the best E. Purple dashed: Lorenz with 107 points. For low-dimensional chaos skill decays with the horizon; for uncorrelated noise it is flat near zero.</p></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embSmap"></div><p class="cap">S-map (Sugihara 1994): one local linear map per prediction, weights exp(−θd/d̄). θ = 0 is a global linear autoregression; skill that rises with θ suggests state-dependent (nonlinear) behaviour.</p></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embSurr"></div><p class="cap">Is the S-map gain more than a linear process would give? The same test on 19 IAAFT surrogates (Schreiber and Schmitz 1996, pyunicorn): same values, nearly the same spectrum, no nonlinear structure. p = 0.05 is the smallest possible value with 19. A pass means "not a stationary linear process": promotions, trends and level shifts can pass too, so it is not proof of deterministic dynamics.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div id="embFcWrap">
      <div class="fig" id="embFc"></div>
      <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--ink)"></i>actual</span><span><i style="background:var(--a2)"></i>simplex (own past)</span><span><i style="background:var(--a5)"></i>pooled analogs</span><span><i style="background:var(--a1)"></i>NHITSx</span><span><i style="background:var(--a3)"></i>Chronos-2 + covariates</span><span><i style="background:var(--muted)"></i>SeasonalNaive</span></div>
      <p class="cap">The test window (shaded) for the selected item. Simplex uses only this item's 93 training weeks; the others are the workshop's reference-run forecasts.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>7 · All {f(P.n)} series</h3>
    <div class="grid2">
      <div><div class="fig" id="embPTau"></div><p class="cap">Delay chosen by the first mutual-information minimum.</p></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embPM"></div><div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--a1)"></i>workshop series</span><span><i style="background:#94a3b8"></i>white noise, same length and τ</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embPRho"></div><p class="cap">Box: middle half; whiskers: 10th to 90th percentile; dashed: the yearly-cycle (green) and Lorenz (purple) references. The best E is picked on the same weeks, so these are optimistic.</p></div>
      <div><div class="fig" id="embPSmap"></div><p class="cap">{f(P.smap_valid)} series with a defined skill (constant stretches give none).</p></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Forecasts on the 14 test weeks</h3>
    <div class="tw" id="embPFc"></div>
    <p class="cap">Mean RMSSE over {f(F.n)} series, scored with the workshop's own <code>bootcamp.score</code> (utilsforecast), lower is better; columns on
      the right split by demand type (count underneath). Simplex: E chosen in-sample per series, then one forecast per horizon from the last training
      week with all 93 weeks as library. Pooled analogs: every series scaled by its training mean, all {pooled ? pooled.L : ''}-week windows of all series as one
      library, the {pooled ? pooled.k : ''} nearest with simplex weights (scikit-learn), window and k chosen on the validation weeks. The other rows are the reference run.</p>
    <p class="take">A series' own embedding loses to "repeat last week": with 93 weeks there are too few close analogs, and an embedding can only
      return values it has already seen. Pooling neighbours across all items helps: it beats Naive on mean RMSSE (and on {pct(F.pooled_beats_naive)} % of
      series) and ties AutoETS, the same reason global models win on Day 2. It is worse than Naive on intermittent items, and well behind the
      trained global models and the foundation models, which also use prices and promotions.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>What to take away</h3>
    <ul class="feat" style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.2rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.4rem;font-size:.9rem;color:#334155">
      <li><b>The order of the weeks carries information.</b> In {SH ? pct(SH.share_gt) : ''} % of series, past states that look like the current one
        are followed by weeks that look like the real next week more often than with the same values shuffled. That is the minimum a forecast needs.</li>
      <li><b>The patterns are shared across items.</b> Windows from unrelated products, scaled to their own level, are often close to an item's recent
        weeks, and what followed them sketches its next weeks. One item has about 50 comparable past states; the panel has {AN ? f(AN.n_windows) : ''}.
        This is a picture of why global models, trained on windows of every series, can forecast items whose own history looks like noise.</li>
      <li><b>The most visible pattern comes from outside.</b> Promotion weeks draw the "star" in the portraits and the isolated stripes in the recurrence
        plots. They are not hidden dynamics but the price calendar, which is why the models with price and promotion inputs do best on Day 2.</li>
      <li><b>The assumptions do not hold.</b> Takens needs a deterministic, autonomous, noise-free system and long records. Weekly retail sales
        are driven from outside (prices, promotions, holidays), are counts with many zeros, and give 93 points. Stark (1999) and Stark et al. (2003)
        extend embedding theorems to systems with deterministic and stochastic forcing, but they need the forcing to be generic and say nothing
        about how much data is enough.</li>
      <li><b>Ties break the tools.</b> Integer sales repeat exactly, so many delay vectors coincide. We had to break ties with a tiny jitter before
        mutual information (its rank step otherwise orders tied zeros by time) and false neighbours (otherwise 0/0).</li>
      <li><b>Check the package against the paper.</b> teaspoon's FNN counts a neighbour as false only if it passes Kennel's second test, and stops at
        10 %, so white noise of this length "embeds" in 3 dimensions. We used neurokit2's implementation, which flags a neighbour if either test
        fires (R<sub>tol</sub> = 10, A<sub>tol</sub> = 2, R<sub>A</sub> = standard deviation), as in Kennel et al.</li>
      <li><b>As a single-series method it is weak here.</b> Choosing τ and m is unreliable with 93 noisy weeks, and forecasting an item from its own
        embedding is worse than repeating last week. {pct(P.smap_sig_all)} % of series pass the S-map nonlinearity test (5 % by chance), which says
        "not linear and stationary" more than "deterministic". Aggregates and the synthetic references show what clean structure would look like.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="plate">
    <h3>Sources and code</h3>
    <ul class="feat" style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.2rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.25rem;font-size:.85rem;color:#334155">
      <li>F. Takens (1981), Detecting strange attractors in turbulence. Lecture Notes in Mathematics 898, 366–381.</li>
      <li>N. Packard, J. Crutchfield, J. D. Farmer, R. Shaw (1980), Geometry from a time series. Phys. Rev. Lett. 45, 712.</li>
      <li>T. Sauer, J. Yorke, M. Casdagli (1991), Embedology. J. Stat. Phys. 65, 579–616 (m &gt; 2 × box-counting dimension, for almost every observation).</li>
      <li>A. Fraser, H. Swinney (1986), Independent coordinates for strange attractors from mutual information. Phys. Rev. A 33, 1134.</li>
      <li>M. Kennel, R. Brown, H. Abarbanel (1992), Determining embedding dimension for phase-space reconstruction using a geometrical construction. Phys. Rev. A 45, 3403.</li>
      <li>J.-P. Eckmann, S. Kamphorst, D. Ruelle (1987), Recurrence plots of dynamical systems. Europhys. Lett. 4, 973.</li>
      <li>G. Sugihara, R. May (1990), Nonlinear forecasting as a way of distinguishing chaos from measurement error in time series. Nature 344, 734.</li>
      <li>G. Sugihara (1994), Nonlinear forecasting for the classification of natural time series. Phil. Trans. R. Soc. A 348, 477.</li>
      <li>T. Schreiber, A. Schmitz (1996), Improved surrogate data for nonlinearity tests. Phys. Rev. Lett. 77, 635.</li>
      <li>J. Stark (1999), Delay embeddings for forced systems I. J. Nonlinear Sci. 9, 255; J. Stark, D. Broomhead, M. Davies, J. Huke (2003), … II. Stochastic forcing. J. Nonlinear Sci. 13, 519.</li>
      <li>Packages: teaspoon (mutual information), neurokit2 (false nearest neighbours), pyEDM (simplex, S-map), pyunicorn (IAAFT surrogates), scikit-learn (pooled analogs), utilsforecast (RMSSE). Recurrence plots and DET are computed in your browser.</li>
      <li>Notebook: <code>08_bonus_delay_embeddings</code> in the Day 2 workshop materials reproduces this page's analysis on all series (about 5 minutes on 4 CPU cores; needs pyEDM, teaspoon, neurokit2, pyunicorn, scikit-learn and joblib).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
